6. OBSERVATIONS:
- Single-threaded (s): Scales from 21,854.35 req/s at c=1 to 65,221.79 req/s at c=64.
- Thread-per-request (m): Peaks at 30,460.09 req/s at c=4 and degrades to ~23,691.66 req/s at c=128.
- Thread Pool 1 (t1): Reaches 58,522.38 req/s at c=16 and 62,197.87 req/s at c=128.
- Thread Pool 2 (t2): Scales smoothly to 66,061.71 req/s at c=64 and 67,639.25 req/s at c=128.
- Thread Pool 4 (t4): Peaks at 75,442.15 req/s at c=128 with latency of 0.920 ms.
- Thread Pool 8 (t8): Peaks at 73,824.74 req/s at c=64 and 73,281.00 req/s at c=128 with latency of 1.070 ms.
- Response Time: Increases roughly linearly with concurrency beyond c=8. Thread pools with 4 and 8 workers consistently demonstrate lower latencies than single-threaded and thread-per-request modes.

7. EXPLANATION & INFERENCES:
- Intermediate Workload Characteristics: /square involves query parsing (strtok_r, strtoll) and arithmetic formatting (snprintf), falling between CPU-heavy prime calculation and pure I/O pingpong.
- Architecture Comparison: The fixed thread pool model decisively beats thread-per-request (75k req/s vs 24k req/s) due to the complete avoidance of per-request thread allocation overhead.
- Multi-core Utilization: Multi-worker thread pools (t4, t8) deliver ~15-20% higher peak throughput than single-threaded by parallelizing parsing, socket I/O, and HTTP formatting across the 2 allocated cores.
